    "dsa-interview-patterns": { notes: ["Clarify input bounds and edge cases, then state a simple baseline before optimizing.", "Common patterns include hash lookup, two pointers, sliding windows, binary search, BFS/DFS, and dynamic programming.", "Explain the invariant and complexity, then test the algorithm on a small example and boundary cases."], questions: makeQuestions("dsa-interview-patterns", [["Easy", "Before optimizing a coding solution, a good first step is to:", ["Clarify constraints and edge cases", "Start typing without reading", "Assume input is sorted", "Ignore the return format"], 0, "Constraints and edge cases determine which algorithms are valid and help avoid incorrect assumptions."], ["Medium", "A sliding window is most directly useful when:", ["A contiguous range can be adjusted while maintaining a condition", "The input has no order and all pairs are needed", "Every node must be sorted", "Only recursion is allowed"], 0, "A movable window can update range state incrementally, often reducing repeated work."], ["Medium", "When presenting complexity, include:", ["Time and auxiliary space", "Only the number of lines", "The interviewer’s typing speed", "The language version only"], 0, "Time and extra-space bounds explain the efficiency and memory cost of the approach."]]) },
